"""pktrec-tb - timebase discipline and calibration of the FPGA Packet Recorder.

The capture block (include/pktrec_regs.h PKTREC_CAP_*, 0x4_2003_0000) holds one
64-bit nanosecond timebase shared by all four ports: it advances by INC (32.32
ns, TB_INC_NS/TB_INC_FRAC) on every dp_clk cycle (249.975 MHz), and software can
SET it, ADJ (add a signed step to) it and read it (TB_NOW). Every capture record
and every PHY SFD timestamp comes from it. pktrec-tb keeps it on CLOCK_REALTIME
(which chronyd keeps on NTP through the PS Ethernet port) so that
timestamps are absolute wall-clock times, and it loads and saves the persistent
calibration (timebase frequency, MAC_OFFSET, SFD windows and per-port offsets).

Commands:
  status [--json]          timebase vs CLOCK_REALTIME now (offset, INC, ppm vs
                           nominal), NTP state, and the daemon's state if it runs
  set [--force]            one-shot TB_SET from CLOCK_REALTIME (what pktrec-cap
                           does at start); refused while a capture runs
  trim (--ppm X | --ppb X | --inc 0xNNNNNNNNNN) [--force]
                           write INC (frequency); a running daemon adopts it
  daemon [options]         discipline the timebase to CLOCK_REALTIME
                           (pktrec-tb.service); state in /run/pktrec/tb.json
  apply-calibration [--file F] [--dry-run] [--force] [--no-timebase]
                           write /etc/pktrec/calibration.conf to the registers
                           (pktrec-calibration.service, at boot)
  save-calibration [--file F | --stdout] [--defaults] [--min-locked MIN]
                   [--max-ppm-delta PPM] [--force-timebase]
                           write the current register values (or the design
                           defaults) as a calibration file. The timebase ppm is
                           the daemon's frequency estimate, stored only when the
                           daemon has been locked for >= --min-locked (10) minutes
                           and the estimate moved < --max-ppm-delta (0.2) ppm over
                           that time; otherwise the file's existing ppm is kept
                           (and that is said). The SFD RX offsets are stored
                           without the watch's per-link-up 0x0055 correction.
  show-calibration [--file F] [--json]
                           registers vs the calibration file, key by key

Daemon (controller, see docs/source/software.md "Timebase discipline"):
  every --interval s (1): read TB_NOW between two CLOCK_REALTIME reads --samples
  times (7) and keep the pair with the shortest read latency:
      offset = TB_NOW - (t1 + t2) / 2         (> 0: the timebase is ahead)
  start-up: TB_SET (+ one ADJ for the residual). If the calibration set the
  frequency (INC != nominal, pktrec-calibration.service), the controller starts
  from it (seeded integrator) and tracks at once. Otherwise (or --estimate-always)
  --estimate s (8) of free running estimate the frequency error (least-squares
  slope), INC corrected, TB_SET again. Then a PI controller on the frequency:
      freq_ppb = I - kp * offset / dt,   I += -ki * offset / dt
  (kp 0.2, ki 0.02; |freq| <= --max-ppm 200, with anti-windup), written as
  INC = nominal * (1 + freq_ppb * 1e-9), nominal = 2^32 * 1e9 / DP_CLK_HZ.
  TB_ADJ steps only when |offset| > --adj-threshold (20 us) and no capture
  runs, at most --max-adj (1 us) per interval. TB_SET only at start-up and when
  |offset| > --step-threshold (1 s, e.g. NTP stepped the system clock), never
  while a capture runs. While a capture runs the phase is corrected through the
  frequency only (an INC change moves a frame waiting in a FIFO by
  cycles x dINC, picoseconds; a TB_ADJ step would move it by the step).
  Another user of the timebase (e.g. replay, which releases frames against it)
  holds steps off the same way by creating $PKTREC_RUN_DIR/tb-hold.<pid>
  (ignored once that process is gone) or tb-hold (until removed).
  locked = |offset| < --lock-threshold (10 us) for --lock-time (30 s); lost
  after 3 samples with |offset| > 5 x the threshold, or after a SET / step.
  While locked, the frequency estimate (integrator) of the last --stable-window
  s (600) is kept as min / max / mean in the state file: save-calibration uses it.

  The timebase follows CLOCK_REALTIME, so its frequency follows the kernel's:
  the trim converges to (system clock frequency correction) + (timebase
  oscillator vs PS crystal, ~0.06 ppm on the bench). The kernel's correction
  (adjtimex freq + tick, set by chronyd) is logged next to the trim in the
  minute summary and in 'status'; an untrained kernel (right after the first
  boot, before chronyd has a drift file) makes both wrong by the PS crystal's
  error (bench: -11.7 ppm) for a few minutes.

All commands that touch the hardware map the capture block through generic-uio
(the Regs helper of pktrec-phy, imported from the same directory) or /dev/mem
(--devmem). --sim <ppm> replaces the hardware with a simulated timebase whose
oscillator is off by <ppm> (host testing; registers in $PKTREC_RUN_DIR).
"""

